    "summaryAr": "Program Overview The ClickHouse PhD Research Internship Program offers doctoral students the opportunity to pursue ambitious research alongside ClickHouse Labs researchers and engineers working on some of the most interesting challenges in databases, systems, and data infrastructure. Research interns join ClickHouse Labs for approximately 10-12 weeks to explore meaningful research questions, develop new ideas, and in",
    "descriptionAr": "Program Overview The ClickHouse PhD Research Internship Program offers doctoral students the opportunity to pursue ambitious research alongside ClickHouse Labs researchers and engineers working on some of the most interesting challenges in databases, systems, and data infrastructure. Research interns join ClickHouse Labs for approximately 10-12 weeks to explore meaningful research questions, develop new ideas, and investigate technologies that have the potential to advance both the broader research community and the future of ClickHouse. The program is intentionally designed around research collaboration . Interns are paired with experienced researchers and have the opportunity to pursue work that aligns with their academic interests, ongoing research, and areas of interest within ClickHouse Labs. In some cases, an internship may build on research, a prototype, or a system the student has already begun exploring at their university. We believe the strongest research relationships extend beyond a single summer. Interns may continue their work after returning to their university through academic research, open-source contributions, publications, or other forms of collaboration. Where there is a strong mutual fit, an internship may become the beginning of a longer-term relationship between the researcher, ClickHouse Labs, and the broader academic community. During their internship, researchers will have the opportunity to work closely with ClickHouse Labs, engage with ClickHouse's open-source technology and engineering community, share their findings, and contribute to the intellectual life of the broader research community. Program length: 10-12 weeks, with flexibility based on academic calendars and research needs Typical timing: Summer, beginning approximately June 1 Locations: Opportunities available across the US and Europe, subject to role and employment requirements. Remote first. Research environment: ClickHouse Labs Internship format: Research-focused, mentor-supported, and shaped around areas of mutual research interest Compensation: commensurate with experience, a monthly stipend will be paid Research at ClickHouse Labs ClickHouse Labs explores ambitious ideas at the intersection of databases, distributed systems, data infrastructure, and the broader systems research community. We believe some of the most interesting advances in these areas happen through close collaboration between industry and academia. ClickHouse Labs brings together researchers and engineers to explore new ideas, investigate challenging technical problems, and connect academic research with real-world systems at scale. Research interns are an important part of that community. Interns have the opportunity to bring their own research interests and perspectives into ClickHouse Labs while working alongside researchers and engineers who are deeply engaged with both the practical challenges and open research questions shaping the future of data systems. Program Goals Identify exceptional PhD researchers working in areas directly relevant to ClickHouse. Give researchers the opportunity to work on meaningful, open-ended problems with ClickHouse Labs. Build long-term relationships with researchers and academic institutions. Encourage continued research and open-source contributions after the internship. Create opportunities for co-authored papers, thesis advising, or other academic collaboration. Increase ClickHouse's visibility within the database and systems research community. Publish and share meaningful research outcomes through the ClickHouse website and other appropriate channels. What Makes the Program Different This is intentionally not a highly structured engineering internship and should not detailed planning. ****But the high-level problem area and expectations should defined several months before the internship begins. This flexibility is intentional and allows ClickHouse Labs to respond to: Emerging research questions Current ClickHouse priorities New academic research Interesting systems or database prototypes Work candidates have already completed Opportunities to apply new research ideas to ClickHouse The goal is to identify researchers whose work is already closely aligned with areas ClickHouse cares about and then determine the most valuable research problem to pursue together. Candidate Profile We are looking for exceptional PhD students conducting research directly relevant to ClickHouse and the broader database/systems space. Ideal candidates Currently pursuing a PhD in computer science or a closely related field Conducting research in areas relevant to ClickHouse Demonstrated research excellence Strong database systems, distributed systems, hardware, or related technical background Has demonstrated ability to independently pursue complex research questions Ideally has prior experience with ClickHouse open source but not required**.** Has research, prototypes, or publications that could translate into meaningful work with ClickHouse Interested in continuing research beyond the internship Potential interest in open-source contribution and academic collaboration Candidate Selection Philosophy The strongest candidates will be researchers whose work is specifically relevant to what ClickHouse Labs wants to explore. A candidate who has already: Worked with ClickHouse Built a relevant database or systems prototype Published research in an area relevant to ClickHouse Explored a technical problem ClickHouse is interested in may be significantly more valuable than a candidate with an otherwise impressive academic profile but no meaningful connection to our research areas. Research Areas & Project Scope Unlike an engineering internship program, specific projects do not need to be defined when recruiting begins. Research projects may emerge from: A candidate's existing research A recent academic paper A prototype or system the candidate has developed An emerging ClickHouse Labs research priority An opportun",
